WEBVTT

resistance-06-1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:07.129
Previously: she had a card she wrote
herself, but it still had no place in the

resistance-06-2
00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:07.490
club.

resistance-06-3
00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:14.970
This time: what exactly does ‘no place’
mean? What does recognition cost?

resistance-06-4
00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.710
Episode 6: You Must First Learn the Other
Side’s Language

resistance-06-5
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:31.683
Turn over any assigned card in the club.
On the back are four boxes, already

resistance-06-6
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:32.850
filled in.

resistance-06-7
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:38.114
Who, when, how to interact with others,
and where one ranks in the hierarchy.

resistance-06-8
00:00:38.114 --> 00:00:39.550
This is a ‘position.’

resistance-06-9
00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.551
On the card she wrote herself, all four
boxes are empty. It is not poorly

resistance-06-10
00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:47.840
written. Only the order can fill in those
boxes.

resistance-06-11
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.449
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): an assigned
identity already carries its position and

resistance-06-12
00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:53.091
relations within the order—‘who, when and
how to interact, and in what hierarchy it

resistance-06-13
00:00:53.091 --> 00:00:55.350
is situated.’ The self-image created by a
dissenting group lacks these.

resistance-06-14
00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.450
So she is caught between two sides.

resistance-06-15
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:06.420
Reject more, and the card is more like
her—but the club does not recognize it.

resistance-06-16
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.434
Reject less, and the club recognizes
it—but it is no longer the self she wants

resistance-06-17
00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:11.820
to be.

resistance-06-18
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.753
The dissenting group’s anxiety: making
its self-image satisfying (rejecting part

resistance-06-19
00:01:16.753 --> 00:01:21.500
of the assigned identity), while also
making it recognized (not rejecting too

resistance-06-20
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:21.870
much).

resistance-06-21
00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.700
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): ‘anxious
about making the self-image satisfying

resistance-06-22
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.530
(by rejecting part of the assigned
identity) while also making it recognized

resistance-06-23
00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:28.610
(by not rejecting too much).’

resistance-06-24
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.202
The first time she approaches the
president, she says: ‘I want to dance my

resistance-06-25
00:01:34.202 --> 00:01:34.870
own dance.’

resistance-06-26
00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:39.314
The president says: ‘There is no such
item on the roster.’ It is not

resistance-06-27
00:01:39.314 --> 00:01:43.040
unreasonableness; the roster’s boxes have
no word for it.

resistance-06-28
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.058
The second time, she puts it differently:
‘The club’s style × new elements—a fusion

resistance-06-29
00:01:48.058 --> 00:01:50.960
piece that could score points in a
competition.’

resistance-06-30
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:58.560
The same dance, described in the club’s
own words, now has a place on the roster.

resistance-06-31
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.152
To be recognized, a radical self-image
must be translated into an acceptable

resistance-06-32
00:02:07.152 --> 00:02:10.020
agenda—using the order’s own grammar.

resistance-06-33
00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.814
Author’s OSIPP draft, 2B(i): dissenting
groups translate fairly radical

resistance-06-34
00:02:11.814 --> 00:02:13.784
self-images into acceptable agendas,
seeking recognition through the political

resistance-06-35
00:02:13.784 --> 00:02:15.300
order’s grammar (the author cites Ferree
2003, pp. 339–340).

resistance-06-36
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.401
This is also an intuition the author
brings to gender studies: often, those

resistance-06-37
00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:25.502
criticizing an order must first borrow
its language to make their criticism

resistance-06-38
00:02:25.502 --> 00:02:25.910
heard.

resistance-06-39
00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.250
The author’s intuition

resistance-06-40
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:36.127
Pause for a moment: have you ever changed
how you put something so you could be

resistance-06-41
00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:40.660
heard? After changing it, was it still
what you wanted to say?

resistance-06-42
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.595
Guardrail 1: translation does not mean
surrender. You can borrow the other

resistance-06-43
00:02:44.595 --> 00:02:47.520
side’s language to say things it does not
want to hear.

resistance-06-44
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.658
Guardrail 2: this is no one’s fault. The
order draws the boxes. Anyone seeking a

resistance-06-45
00:02:52.658 --> 00:02:56.640
place on the roster must first learn how
to write within them.

resistance-06-46
00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.580
This episode’s comparison card (take a
screenshot)

resistance-06-47
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.469
Position — Four boxes on the card’s back.
Who, when, how to interact, and where one

resistance-06-48
00:03:04.469 --> 00:03:04.750
ranks.

resistance-06-49
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.477
No position — A self-written card with
four empty boxes. Only the order can fill

resistance-06-50
00:03:08.477 --> 00:03:08.850
them in.

resistance-06-51
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.150
Anxiety in both directions — Reject too
much: no recognition. Too little: it is

resistance-06-52
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.030
not me. It must be satisfying and
recognized.

resistance-06-53
00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.559
Translation — The ‘fusion piece’ gets on
the roster. Speaking one’s own words

resistance-06-54
00:03:19.559 --> 00:03:21.570
through the order’s grammar.

resistance-06-55
00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.840
She is on the roster.

resistance-06-56
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.290
But which card made it onto the roster?

resistance-06-57
00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.080
The chair is still there.

resistance-06-58
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.740
Next time:

resistance-06-59
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:40.190
Did Those Who Created the Anxiety Relieve
It?

resistance-06-60
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:47.400
This is UjiBricks.
